const e={note:"Eight currents for the Aramaic hall. Aramaic is the one language in the Scriptorium that served Jews, Christians, Mandaeans, Manichaeans and pagans at the same time, and its esoteric literature has to be read across those lines rather than sorted between them. Native strings are given in Hebrew square script for Jewish Aramaic, and in the Mandaic alphabet in the Mandaean current, where transliteration follows Drower and Macuch. Three qualifications on the native fields. In the Early Jewish Mysticism current the technical vocabulary is Hebrew rather than Aramaic, since that is the language of the Hekhalot corpus itself, and the glosses say so. One term elsewhere is likewise marked as Hebrew. And the Aramaic words embedded in the Greek New Testament survive only in Greek letters; the Hebrew-script forms given here are scholarly retroversions, and are labelled as such. These essays describe historically what these texts say and what their practitioners understood themselves to be doing. They are not procedures, and no operative sequence, formulary or adjuration is reproduced.",themes:[{slug:"incantation-bowls",title:"The Incantation Bowls",native:"אסותא מן שמיא",translit:"asuta min shemayya",summary:"Inscribed clay bowls from Sasanian and early Islamic Mesopotamia, written in an ink spiral and, where the archaeology was recorded, buried upside down beneath the floors of ordinary houses.",paragraphs:["The object is disarmingly plain: a wheel-thrown earthenware bowl of the kind one ate from, undecorated, the sort of vessel a household owned by the dozen. What makes it a magical instrument is the ink. The text runs in a spiral, usually from the centre outward to the rim, though the reverse direction also occurs, as do concentric rings and arrangements divided into wedges. At the centre of many bowls sits a drawing: a figure rendered frontally, hair unbound, arms and ankles shackled, the fetters sometimes explicitly labelled. The corpus is generally dated from about the fifth to the eighth century of the common era, which is to say it straddles the fall of the Sasanian empire and continues under early Islamic rule with no visible break in technique, though both ends of that range are argued and few bowls can be dated closely on internal evidence.","The archaeology matters more than it is usually allowed to. The bowls recovered at Nippur during the University of Pennsylvania's campaigns between 1888 and 1900 were recorded in situ, and the pattern they establish governs how the whole corpus is read: most of those documented in position were mouth down, set into the floor of a room, at its corners, in the courtyard, or at the threshold of a door, and in some instances two bowls were found set rim to rim and sealed. Inversion appears to be the operative gesture. The bowl is a trap laid across the boundary of a dwelling, and the boundary is architectural before it is metaphysical. Other bowls come from Kish, Babylon and Borsippa, Uruk, Seleucia-Ctesiphon and Khouabir. But a large share of the corpus is unprovenanced, having surfaced on the antiquities market, most conspicuously in the Schøyen Collection, whose bowls have been the subject of sustained dispute over their acquisition; losses from Iraqi institutions after 2003 have compounded the problem. For a great many bowls we possess a text with no house attached to it, and that is a permanent loss rather than an inconvenience.","James Alan Montgomery's Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, published by the University Museum in Philadelphia in 1913 as the third volume of the Publications of the Babylonian Section, remains the foundation. It edited forty texts with a rigour that had not previously been brought to the material, and its numbering is still the currency of citation. The line of work that follows is continuous: Joseph Naveh and Shaul Shaked's two volumes of 1985 and 1993, Dan Levene's corpus of 2003, the first volume of Aramaic Bowl Spells by Shaked, James Nathan Ford and Siam Bhayro in 2013, Marco Moriggi's corpus of the Syriac bowls in 2014, and Christa Müller-Kessler's long labour on the Mandaic and Syriac material, including her edition of the Nippur bowls in the Hilprecht Collection at Jena. Henri Pognon had already published Mandaic bowls from Khouabir, near Baghdad, in 1898. Even so, a substantial fraction of known bowls has never been edited at all, and estimates of how many exist vary widely because so many sit uncatalogued in private hands.","Four communities wrote them, each in its own hand. Jewish Babylonian Aramaic in the Hebrew square script accounts for the bulk; Syriac appears in Estrangela and in a related hand that Müller-Kessler has labelled proto-Manichaean, a classification not universally accepted; Mandaic bowls use the Mandaean alphabet; and a small group is written in the Manichaean script proper, though whether the content of those bowls is Manichaean in any doctrinal sense is a separate and open question. Then there are the pseudo-inscriptions, bowls covered in a convincing but meaningless imitation of writing. In a society where literacy was the possession of a small minority, these are among the most instructive objects in the corpus, since they suggest that for at least part of the clientele the efficacy lay in the fact of writing rather than in anything the writing said.","The clients are the corpus's quiet gift to social history. Bowls overwhelmingly identify the beneficiary by his or her mother rather than father, a matronymic convention shared with later Jewish amulet practice, though patronymics do occur and the rule is a strong tendency rather than an absolute. The onomastic mixture is remarkable: Jewish names beside Iranian ones, Akkadian survivals, Aramaic names throughout, Arabic names in the later material, and a family of Iranian women's names ending in the element duxt, daughter. Women appear constantly as clients in their own right, commissioning protection for themselves, their households and their unborn children. These are people who appear in no chronicle and no rabbinic anecdote, and we know their names only because they commissioned a bowl."],terms:[{native:"אסותא מן שמיא",translit:"asuta min shemayya",gloss:"healing from heaven; a very common opening formula, though by no means the only one"},{native:"חספא",translit:"ḥaspa",gloss:"potsherd, earthenware; the determined form of the ḥasaf of Daniel 2:33, and the substance of the bowls themselves"},{native:"אסקופתא",translit:"isqupta",gloss:"threshold; among the commonest recorded places of deposit for an inverted bowl"},{native:"לחשא",translit:"laḥsha",gloss:"an incantation, literally a whispering, from the root shared with Hebrew laḥash"},{native:"ברת",translit:"bart",gloss:"daughter of; bowls usually identify clients by the mother's name, though patronymics also occur"}],texts:[{title:"James A. Montgomery, Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur (1913)",note:"Publications of the Babylonian Section III, University Museum, Philadelphia. Forty texts from a controlled excavation; still the foundational edition and the standard system of reference."},{title:"Joseph Naveh and Shaul Shaked, Amulets and Magic Bowls (1985)",note:"With its companion volume Magic Spells and Formulae (1993), the edition that reopened the field and set bowls beside metal amulets and Geniza fragments."},{title:"Shaul Shaked, James Nathan Ford and Siam Bhayro, Aramaic Bowl Spells: Jewish Babylonian Aramaic Bowls, Volume One (2013)",note:"Schøyen Collection bowls with full philological commentary; also the volume in which the question of acquisition and provenance is hardest to set aside."},{title:"Marco Moriggi, A Corpus of Syriac Incantation Bowls (2014)",note:"The Christian Aramaic side of the corpus gathered and edited, putting cross-confessional comparison on a firm textual basis."},{title:"Christa Müller-Kessler, Die Zauberschalentexte in der Hilprecht-Sammlung, Jena (2005)",note:"Nippur bowls in a collection with an excavation history, edited by the scholar who has done most for the Mandaic and Syriac material."},{title:"Henri Pognon, Inscriptions mandaïtes des coupes de Khouabir (1898)",note:"The first substantial publication of Mandaic bowls, from a site near Baghdad; pre-dates Montgomery and showed early that the practice crossed communities."}]},{slug:"mandaean-scripture",title:"Mandaean Scripture",native:"ࡂࡉࡍࡆࡀ ࡓࡁࡀ",translit:"Ginza Rba",summary:"The books, the baptism and the death-liturgy of a religion of late-antique Mesopotamian origin whose community, priesthood and manuscript tradition have carried it into the present day.",paragraphs:["The Mandaeans take their name from manda, knowledge, and among the religious movements that scholarship has gathered under the label Gnostic they are the one with a living community, though that label is an outside category and not one the tradition uses of itself. Where Valentinians and Sethians are known to us through their opponents and through a library buried in Egypt, the Mandaeans have priests ordained by priests, a manuscript tradition whose colophons record their own chains of copyists, a script that has never fallen out of use, and a liturgy performed this year. They belong to the marshes and river towns of southern Iraq and to Khuzestan in Iran. Their tradition is not a reconstruction and not a revival, and it should not be called a survival either, which makes a community sound like a relic.","The principal scripture is the Ginza Rba, the Great Treasure, also called the Sidra Rba. It falls into two parts: the Right Ginza, carrying cosmology, myth, ethics and the making of the world and of Adam, and the Left Ginza, which concerns death and the ascent of the soul. They are bound in a single codex, and the Left is written so that the reader must turn the book over and begin from the other end, so that the physical volume performs the reversal its subject describes. Petermann issued a lithographic edition in 1867; Lidzbarski's German translation of 1925 remains the version most scholarship works from. The Book of John, the Draša d-Yahia, was edited by Lidzbarski in two parts, text in 1905 and translation in 1915, and received a full critical edition, translation and commentary from Charles Häberl and James McGrath in 2020. The Qulasta, the canonical prayerbook, was published by E. S. Drower in 1959; its core is the Sidra ḏ-Nišmata, the Book of Souls. Drower's decades of work with the community also gave us the Thousand and Twelve Questions, the Haran Gawaita and the Book of the Zodiac.","The doctrine is set out here as the community holds it. Above all is Hiia Rba, the Great Life, known also in the community's own pronunciation as Hayyi Rabbi, in the alma ḏ-nhura, the World of Light. From the Life proceeds Manda ḏ-Hiia, the Knowledge of Life, the messenger who descends and redeems. Below stand Abatur, who weighs souls, and Ptahil, who fashions the material world; Ruha, the spirit, and her seven planetary children belong to the lower order. John the Baptist, Yahia Yuhana, is the honoured prophet and the great baptiser. The Mandaean scriptures, formed in the confessional disputes of late antiquity, regard Jesus as a false messiah who received the baptism and altered it, and Christianity accordingly as a departure from the true baptism. That is recorded here because it is what the texts say; it is not adjudicated, and it should not be taken as a description of how Mandaeans and their Christian neighbours live alongside one another now.","Practice turns on water and on the soul's rising. Maṣbuta, baptism, takes place in a iardna, meaning any flowing water, since flowing water is what makes a Jordan; it is administered by a tarmida, and it is not a once-for-all initiation but a recurrent rite, performed on Sunday and at the great festivals and at the turning points of a life. Masiqta, the raising, is the liturgy conducted for the soul's ascent past the maṭarata, the watch-houses that lie along the way. The priesthood has three grades: tarmida, ganzibra and rišama. The white ritual garment is the rasta, the banner set beside the water is the drabša, and the reed-and-mud ritual house built on the bank is the mandi, the bit manda. The alphabet itself is held in reverence: Mandaic writes its vowels with full letters, which is unusual among Semitic scripts, and the abagada is learned as something more than an inventory of signs.","The community is now in serious difficulty, and there is no way to write about Mandaean scripture honestly without saying so. The draining of the southern marshes in the early 1990s struck at the landscape the tradition presupposes. After 2003 the Iraqi Mandaeans, who had been estimated in the tens of thousands, were reduced by killing, kidnapping and flight, and most estimates now put those remaining in Iraq at a few thousand. A diaspora has formed in Sweden, Australia, Germany, the Netherlands and the United States, where the difficulty is not violence but distance, dispersal and a shortage of priests, since ordination requires priests to perform it. In Iran the pressures are of another kind, chiefly the absence of secure legal standing. And there is a threat that bears directly on the liturgy: the Tigris, the Euphrates and the Karun are polluted, dammed and drawn down, so that flowing water fit for maṣbuta is becoming scarce in the very landscape the rite assumes."],terms:[{native:"ࡄࡉࡉࡀ ࡓࡁࡀ",translit:"Hiia Rba",gloss:"the Great Life, the supreme God; transliteration follows Drower and Macuch, and the community's own pronunciation is Hayyi Rabbi"},{native:"ࡂࡉࡍࡆࡀ ࡓࡁࡀ",translit:"Ginza Rba",gloss:"the Great Treasure, the principal scripture, in Right and Left halves bound in one inverted codex"},{native:"ࡌࡀࡍࡃࡀ ࡖࡄࡉࡉࡀ",translit:"Manda ḏ-Hiia",gloss:"Knowledge of Life; the messenger who descends from the World of Light"},{native:"ࡌࡀࡑࡁࡅࡕࡀ",translit:"maṣbuta",gloss:"baptism in flowing water; repeated throughout life, not a single initiation. Spelled with ṣade, hence maṣbuta rather than masbuta"},{native:"ࡌࡀࡎࡉࡒࡕࡀ",translit:"masiqta",gloss:"the raising; the liturgy performed for the ascent of the soul"},{native:"ࡒࡅࡋࡀࡎࡕࡀ",translit:"Qulasta",gloss:"the canonical prayerbook, whose core is the Sidra ḏ-Nišmata, the Book of Souls"},{native:"ࡉࡀࡓࡃࡍࡀ",translit:"iardna",gloss:"a Jordan; any flowing water, and therefore any water fit for baptism"},{native:"ࡕࡀࡓࡌࡉࡃࡀ",translit:"tarmida",gloss:"priest; the first of the three grades, below ganzibra and rišama"}],texts:[{title:"Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā: Der Schatz oder das grosse Buch der Mandäer (1925)",note:"The standard translation of the Great Treasure, Right and Left; a century old and still the version most scholarship works from."},{title:"Mark Lidzbarski, Das Johannesbuch der Mandäer (1905, 1915)",note:"Text in the first part, German translation in the second; the discourses centred on John the Baptist."},{title:"Charles G. Häberl and James F. McGrath, The Mandaean Book of John: Critical Edition, Translation, and Commentary (2020)",note:"A modern critical edition built on the manuscript tradition, prepared in consultation with members of the community."},{title:"E. S. Drower, The Canonical Prayerbook of the Mandaeans (1959)",note:"The Qulasta in translation, including the masiqta liturgies; the indispensable source for Mandaean prayer."},{title:"E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (1937)",note:"Ethnography and ritual description from years spent with the community; a record of practice that in places no longer exists in the same form."},{title:"Jorunn Jacobsen Buckley, The Great Stem of Souls: Reconstructing Mandaean History",note:"Gorgias Press. The study that turned the manuscript colophons into usable historical evidence for the chains of copyists and priests."}]},{slug:"angelology-demonology",title:"Angelology and Demonology",native:"מלאכין ומזיקין",translit:"malʾakhin u-maziqin",summary:"Who inhabits the bowls: liliths, harmers and demon-kings on one side, chains of angel names on the other, and a striking indifference to whose religion a name came from. The heading is an editorial phrase built from two terms of the corpus.",paragraphs:["A bowl describes a crowded house. The classes of hostile being are enumerated at length and with evident care: shedin and dewin, the latter word Iranian; maziqin, the harmers; liliths male and female; satans; spirits of the dead; and alongside these the harm thought to come from human beings, ḥarshin, sorceries, and ʿovadin bishin, evil works. The lists have the exhaustiveness of a legal instrument's catch-all clause, and that appears to be their function. The scribe is closing every door at once, and an unnamed category is an open door.","Lilith figures large. The bowls know a male lili and a female lilita and speak freely of both in the plural, and the figure has two aspects that overlap: the night visitant who comes to a sleeper and claims to be his wife or her husband, and the destroyer of infants and of women in childbirth. The first aspect is what makes the writ of divorce intelligible as a response. A long ancestry is generally assumed, running back through the Akkadian lilītu and ardat lilî to the lilith who lodges among the ruins in Isaiah 34:14 and to the Arslan Tash plaque conventionally dated to the seventh century before the common era, though the line of descent is not straightforward at any point and the Arslan Tash object has been argued by some scholars to be a modern forgery. What does not belong to this lineage at all is the story of Lilith as Adam's first wife, which appears in the Alphabet of Ben Sira around the ninth or tenth century and is therefore several hundred years later than the bowls. It should not be read back into them, though it constantly is.","The demons have kings. Bagdana is styled king of the demons and ruler of the liliths, and his name looks Iranian. Ashmedai is the Asmodeus of the book of Tobit and of the Babylonian Talmud at Giṭṭin 68, where Solomon binds him and puts him to work on the Temple, a narrative that stands behind a great deal of later magical literature. Danahish appears frequently, often in the company of Bagdana. And there is Nirig, who is Nergal, the Mesopotamian god of war and plague, still present in the bowls after a millennium and a half but demoted, now a demon and a planet. The Mandaean texts complete the demotion by making the seven planets the children of Ruha.","Against them the scribes deploy names. Michael, Gabriel, Raphael, Uriel, Sariel, Nuriel, Qafṣiel and others appear singly and in chains, and the chains run on well past the point of intelligibility into voces magicae, sequences that may be corrupted Greek or Persian, may be deliberately unpronounceable, or may never have carried lexical meaning at all. Accumulation is itself the method; the sequence gathers weight and then terminates in a Name against which no power is thought to have standing. The tone is not always adversarial. Shaked took the title of an important article of 1995 from a bowl that greets the angels as one would greet exalted guests, which is a reminder that the practitioner's posture toward the upper world could be closer to petition and courtesy than to command.","What is most striking is the traffic in names across the lines that theology drew hard. Dan Levene published in 1999 a bowl in Jewish Aramaic, in Hebrew script, that invokes Jesus. Syriac Christian bowls invoke the seal of Solomon and Jewish angel names. Mandaic bowls carry biblical figures. The likeliest explanation is not that the communities were confused about doctrine, for their polemics against one another were precise and often fierce. It is that names were treated as instruments, and an instrument reputed to work was worth having whatever workshop it came from. Part of the borrowing may be simpler still: a scribe of one community writing to order for a client of another, and supplying the names the client asked for. Both accounts are inferences from the texts rather than statements the texts make about themselves."],terms:[{native:"ליליתא",translit:"lilita",gloss:"a lilith, feminine; the masculine lili also occurs, and both are freely pluralised"},{native:"מזיקין",translit:"maziqin",gloss:"harmers; the most general term for damaging spirits, from the root meaning to injure"},{native:"שידין",translit:"shedin",gloss:"demons; the standard class-noun, shared with Hebrew"},{native:"רוחא בישתא",translit:"ruḥa bishta",gloss:"evil spirit; a phrase, not a proper name, and applied very widely"},{native:"אשמדאי",translit:"Ashmedai",gloss:"Asmodeus; named in Tobit, and bound by Solomon in the narrative at Giṭṭin 68a–b"},{native:"בגדנא",translit:"Bagdana",gloss:"a demon-king of the bowls, titled ruler of the liliths; the name appears Iranian in formation"},{native:"דנחיש",translit:"Danahish",gloss:"a demon-king frequently paired with Bagdana; the vocalisation given is conventional and the etymology unsettled"},{native:"מלאכיא",translit:"malʾakhayya",gloss:"the angels; addressed in the bowls sometimes with commands, sometimes with greetings"}],texts:[{title:`Dan Levene, "'... and by the name of Jesus ...': An Unpublished Magic Bowl in Jewish Aramaic" (Jewish Studies Quarterly, 1999)`,note:"The clearest single case of the traffic in names: a Jewish Aramaic bowl invoking Jesus, published with full discussion of what such borrowing does and does not imply."},{title:"Gideon Bohak, Ancient Jewish Magic: A History (2008)",note:"The synthetic account, which treats the bowls' demonology as part of a continuous Jewish tradition rather than a foreign intrusion into it."},{title:"Babylonian Talmud, Giṭṭin 68a–b",note:"Solomon and Ashmedai: the rabbinic narrative that underwrites Solomon's later career across Jewish, Christian and Islamic magical literature."},{title:"Isaiah 34:14",note:"The lilith among the ruins of Edom; the biblical anchor for a figure whose ancestry is Mesopotamian and whose best-known later legend is medieval."},{title:"Alphabet of Ben Sira (c. ninth to tenth century)",note:"The source of the Lilith-as-Adam's-first-wife story. Later than the bowls by centuries and not evidence for them."},{title:"Joseph Naveh and Shaul Shaked, Magic Spells and Formulae (1993)",note:"Amulets, bowls and Geniza texts together, with the demon and angel nomenclature treated comparatively."}]},{slug:"healing-protection",title:"Healing and Protection",native:"נטירותא",translit:"nṭiruta",summary:"What the bowls were largely for: health, sleep, safe childbirth, a sound house and living children — and, in one limited group of texts, the writ of divorce served on a demon.",paragraphs:["The word that opens a great many bowls is asuta, healing. Not gnosis, not ascent, not power over others: health. Read in bulk, the corpus is a record of ordinary trouble in a Mesopotamian village or town. Someone cannot sleep. A woman has lost a succession of children. A man is ill and the illness has not responded. The livestock are dying. A neighbour is thought to have done something. These are not exotic anxieties, and the bowls answer them in the register in which they were felt, which is one reason the corpus resists the older habit of treating magic as the debased end of religion. Nobody commissioned a bowl in order to be interesting.","The scope of protection is spelled out with the thoroughness of a conveyance. A bowl will name the client, then his wife, then his sons and his daughters where they are known, then his house and his dwelling, his threshold, his cattle and his possessions, and sometimes his fields. The body may be itemised limb by limb. The unborn receive particular attention: a recognised group of bowls is written for pregnant women against the lilith who takes infants, asking that the children of this woman should live and not die. In a world of high infant mortality that is not superstition standing in for medicine but a considered response to a danger for which no medicine existed.","Alongside the bowls stand the amulets, qameʿa, and the two share a repertoire. The amulets are thin lamellae of silver, gold, bronze or lead, inscribed in minute script, rolled tightly and carried in a tube at the neck or on the arm; many of the finest come from Palestine and Syria and were edited by Naveh and Shaked. The difference is one of deployment rather than kind: the bowl guards a place and stays there, the amulet guards a body and travels with it. Some bowls call themselves qameʿa outright. Scripture supplies part of the repertoire on both, above all Psalm 91, which rabbinic sources call the song against afflictions, along with the priestly blessing of Numbers 6 and the divine self-designation as healer in Exodus 15:26. That the same page of the Talmud which names the psalm also holds that one may not heal oneself with words of Torah is a tension the tradition never resolved.","The most arresting device in the corpus is the get, the writ of divorce, served upon a demon. It belongs to a distinct and limited group of texts rather than to the normal run of bowls, and its logic is legal rather than magical, which is the point. The lilith is understood to have attached herself to a man as a wife, or to a woman as a husband; the practitioner therefore meets her with the instrument by which a marriage is ended in Jewish law. The bowl follows the general shape of the rabbinic formulary, with its dismissal of the spouse and its extinction of her claims, and on that basis requires the demon to depart and not return. Some bowls reach instead for the shamta, the ban, the community's other legal weapon, here aimed at a party outside the community altogether. The jurisprudential move underneath is what repays attention: the practitioner is not asserting superior force, he is asserting jurisdiction. The demon is treated as a person with standing, capable of being served and therefore capable of being dismissed. The formularies themselves are described here and deliberately not reproduced.","None of this displaced the physician. The asya practised alongside the writer of bowls, and the therapeutic pages of the Babylonian Talmud at Giṭṭin 67b to 70b move between pharmacology, dietetics, regimen and adjuration without any sense of crossing a boundary. The boundary is ours. A patient in sixth-century Babylonia might take a remedy, follow a diet, and set a bowl beneath the threshold, and would have understood all three as prudent measures against the same trouble, differing in mechanism but not in kind."],terms:[{native:"אסותא",translit:"asuta",gloss:"healing; the goal named at the head of a great many bowls"},{native:"נטירותא",translit:"nṭiruta",gloss:"guarding, protection; the companion term to asuta"},{native:"קמיעא",translit:"qameʿa",gloss:"amulet; usually an inscribed metal lamella, though bowls sometimes use the word of themselves"},{native:"גיטא",translit:"giṭṭa",gloss:"writ of divorce; the legal instrument turned against the lilith who claims to be a spouse, in one limited group of bowls"},{native:"שמתא",translit:"shamta",gloss:"ban, excommunication; the other legal weapon deployed against demons"},{native:"ביתא",translit:"bayta",gloss:"house; the unit protected, along with its threshold and its corners"},{native:"בעירא",translit:"beʿira",gloss:"cattle, livestock; named in the protection clause beside the household"},{native:"אסיא",translit:"asya",gloss:"physician; the practitioner working alongside, not against, the writer of bowls"}],texts:[{title:"Joseph Naveh and Shaul Shaked, Amulets and Magic Bowls: Aramaic Incantations of Late Antiquity (1985)",note:"The metal lamellae from Palestine and Syria edited beside Babylonian bowls, which is what makes the shared repertoire visible."},{title:"Dan Levene, A Corpus of Magic Bowls: Incantation Texts in Jewish Aramaic from Late Antiquity (2003)",note:"Some thirty texts including several of the divorce bowls, with attention to the legal formulae the scribes were adapting."},{title:"Shaul Shaked (ed.), Officina Magica: Essays on the Practice of Magic in Antiquity (2005)",note:"Essays on how such texts were composed and used, including the borrowing of legal forms into ritual."},{title:"Babylonian Talmud, Giṭṭin 67b–70b",note:"The long therapeutic passage: remedies, regimen and adjurations set side by side without embarrassment."},{title:"Babylonian Talmud, Shevuʿot 15b",note:"Psalm 91 named as the song against afflictions, on the same page as the ruling that one may not heal oneself with words of Torah."},{title:"Mishnah Giṭṭin",note:"The formulary of the writ of divorce in its proper legal setting, which the bowl-scribes evidently knew and adapted."}]},{slug:"mystical-prayers",title:"Mystical Prayers",native:"יהא שמיה רבא",translit:"yehe shemeh rabba",summary:"Why the tradition's most exalted doxology is in Aramaic, what the Targum does to a verse by translating it, and why a Castilian book of the thirteenth century chose a language nobody spoke.",paragraphs:["The Kaddish is an Aramaic doxology built around a congregational response: may his great name be blessed for ever and to all eternity. The Talmud's valuation of that response is extraordinary. At Berakhot 3a Elijah tells R. Yose that when Israel answer it in their synagogues the Holy One laments the children he has exiled; at Sotah 49a it is named, with the qedushah de-sidra, as one of the two things upon which the world endures; at Shabbat 119b, whoever answers it with all his strength has an evil decree torn up. Its original setting appears to have been the close of study rather than the service of prayer, which survives in the Kaddish de-rabbanan; the mourner's Kaddish is a later development, medieval and Ashkenazi, first firmly attested in the thirteenth-century Or Zaruaʿ and attached to a legend of a dead man relieved by his son's recitation. And there is a paradox at its centre. At Shabbat 12b the Talmud states that the ministering angels do not attend to Aramaic, a dictum invoked for centuries to explain why one should pray in Hebrew. The great doxology is in Aramaic all the same. The usual resolutions are that it was the vernacular and therefore said by everyone, or, more interestingly, that a prayer needing no angelic intermediary is the stronger for it.","Targum was performance before it was literature. The meturgeman stood beside the reader and rendered the lesson aloud verse by verse, and the rules gathered at Megillah 32a insist that he do so without reading from a written text, so that translation should not be mistaken for scripture. Interpretation was therefore built into the liturgy itself. The characteristic device is the buffer-word: Memra, the Word, Yeqara, the Glory, and Shekhinta, the Indwelling, set wherever the Hebrew places God too directly into a body or a place. The corpus is large and various: Onqelos and Targum Jonathan to the Prophets, Pseudo-Jonathan, the Fragment Targums, the Geniza fragments, and Codex Neofiti 1, a manuscript copied in 1504 that sat in the Vatican Library catalogued as Onqelos until Alejandro Díez Macho recognised in 1956 what it actually was. Targums of Job and of Leviticus were found at Qumran, which puts the practice back well before the rabbinic collections. To translate is already to decide, and the Targumim decided constantly.","The Zohar's Aramaic is a made thing. The book presents itself as the teaching of Shimon bar Yohai in second-century Galilee; it was composed in Castile in the later thirteenth century, and its language is an artificial literary Aramaic assembled from Targumic and Talmudic sources, salted with Hebraisms and Arabisms, shaped here and there by Castilian syntax, and containing coinages and grammatical irregularities that betray its date. Scholem set out the argument in the fifth of his Major Trends lectures and attributed the work to Moses de León; Yehuda Liebes's studies of the Zohar's lexicon carried the analysis into the vocabulary itself and then, in later work, into the case for a circle of writers rather than a single author, a view since developed by Ronit Meroz and Daniel Abrams. Single authorship is Scholem's thesis, not a settled fact. The question worth asking is not how the pretence was detected but why Aramaic was chosen. It conferred antiquity. It was the language of the Targum, adjacent to holiness without being the holy tongue. And an invented idiom afforded a register of solemn strangeness that Hebrew could not have afforded, which suited a book about secrecy.","One passage made the return journey into ordinary worship. Berikh Shemeh, from the Vayaqhel section of the Zohar, is recited when the ark is opened and the scroll taken out: the speaker declares that he trusts in no man and relies on no angel, but on the God of heaven. It is standard in Sephardi and Nusaḥ Sefard rites and has been widely adopted elsewhere, though it is not universal and entered some Ashkenazi rites late or not at all. A paragraph of esoteric Aramaic from a thirteenth-century book of secrets became a fixed prayer said aloud by congregations who mostly do not know where it came from. It is the clearest traceable case of the kabbalistic and the liturgical exchanging places.","The Mandaean side of the ledger holds its own ascent-prayers. The Qulasta, whose core is the Sidra ḏ-Nišmata, the Book of Souls, supplies the hymns sung at the masiqta: the soul is addressed as it rises, the watch-houses along its road are named, and the garment of light prepared for it is described. Drower's translation of 1959 opened this literature to readers outside the community. Set the two liturgies beside each other and the resemblance is arresting. Within a few hundred miles, in sibling dialects of one language, two communities whose doctrines stood far apart produced prayer preoccupied with the same two things: the greatness of the Name, and the passage of the soul."],terms:[{native:"קדיש",translit:"Qaddish",gloss:"the Aramaic doxology; apparently first at home at the close of a session of study"},{native:"יהא שמיה רבא",translit:"yehe shemeh rabba",gloss:"may his great name be; the congregational response, named at Sotah 49a among the things on which the world endures"},{native:"בריך שמיה",translit:"Berikh Shemeh",gloss:"blessed be the name; the Zohar passage recited in many rites on taking out the Torah scroll"},{native:"תרגום",translit:"Targum",gloss:"translation; specifically the Aramaic renderings of scripture, and the practice of rendering aloud"},{native:"מתורגמן",translit:"meturgeman",gloss:"the translator who rendered the lesson verse by verse, and who was not to read from a written text"},{native:"מימרא",translit:"Memra",gloss:"the Word; a Targumic buffer-term standing where the Hebrew speaks of God acting directly"},{native:"יקרא",translit:"Yeqara",gloss:"the Glory; a second such buffer-term, used of God's manifest presence"},{native:"שכינתא",translit:"Shekhinta",gloss:"the Indwelling; the Targumic term for God's dwelling in a place, later of great consequence in Kabbalah"}],texts:[{title:"Babylonian Talmud, Berakhot 3a, Sotah 49a and Shabbat 119b",note:"The three passages that give the Kaddish response its standing: Elijah in the ruin, the world enduring upon it, and the decree torn up."},{title:"Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 12b",note:"The statement that the ministering angels do not attend to Aramaic; the dictum against which the Aramaic liturgy has to be read."},{title:"Codex Neofiti 1, Vatican Library",note:"A complete Palestinian Targum of the Pentateuch, copied in 1504 and catalogued as Onqelos until Alejandro Díez Macho identified it in 1956."},{title:"Zohar, Parashat Vayaqhel (II, 206a)",note:"The source of Berikh Shemeh; a passage of the Zohar's artificial Aramaic that entered the standard liturgy."},{title:"Gershom Scholem, Major Trends in Jewish Mysticism (1941), fifth lecture",note:"The classic argument from the Zohar's language to its date; its attribution to a single author has since been much contested."},{title:"E. S. Drower, The Canonical Prayerbook of the Mandaeans (1959)",note:"The Qulasta, including the hymns of the masiqta that accompany the soul past the watch-houses."}]},{slug:"magical-formulae",title:"Late-Antique Magical Formulae",native:"אשבעית עליכון",translit:"ashbaʿit ʿalekhon",summary:"The anatomy of a spell: a myth cited as precedent, an adjuration that binds by something higher, a seal, and an amen. Described here structurally, as the scholarship describes it, and not as procedure.",paragraphs:["The corpus has an anatomy, and it is remarkably stable across four scripts and three religions. A text opens with an invocation or a heading; it narrates a precedent; it adjures; it seals; it closes with amen. The parts are modular, and the same block recurs in bowls separated by a century and several hundred miles, which is the strongest reason to think that handbooks circulated long before the period from which any survive. For the later period we have the handbooks themselves: the magical formularies of the Cairo Geniza, edited by Naveh and Shaked and studied by Bohak, show the model books from which individual texts were assembled, with slots left open for a client's name.","The historiola is a myth in miniature, told not for its own sake but as precedent. What happened once must happen now. The bowls' favourite concerns Joshua bar Peraḥia, a sage of the second or first century before the common era, who is said in these texts to have issued a ban against the liliths; a bowl cites that ruling much as an advocate cites a case, and the demons are held to be bound by it. The attribution is pseudepigraphic — the historical sage has no connection to Sasanian Babylonia — and that is precisely the mechanism. The logic is not narrative but precedential. The story functions as a citation, and the practitioner's authority is derivative: he is not making new law against the demon, he is enforcing a judgement he claims already exists.","The adjuration proper is signalled by a verb from the root š-b-ʿ, which also yields the words for oath and for seven. I adjure you: the practitioner does not command in his own person, and the entire force of the formula lies in the clause that follows the word by. Hence the chains of names, which are not decoration but the substance of the claim. The scribes are careful about grammar here in a way that repays attention, addressing the demon in the correct gender and number, and sometimes covering both in case the identification is wrong. To adjure the wrong sort of being would be, on this logic, like serving papers on the wrong party.","Sealing closes the operation. The Aramaic verb is ḥatam, and its natural companion is ʿizqta, the signet-ring; the two appear together in Daniel 6:18, where the king seals the stone over the lions' den with his signet so that nothing in the matter should be altered. The metaphor is bureaucratic before it is occult: the demon is shut as a document is shut, and the seal most often invoked is the ring of Solomon, whose career as master of the demons runs from the Greek Testament of Solomon — a pseudepigraphon whose date is disputed across several centuries — through the Talmud into Islamic tradition as the khātam Sulaymān. The closing tag, amen amen selah, is Hebrew even at the end of an Aramaic text, and belongs to the liturgy rather than to the spell.","This last point generalises. Hebrew divine names sit inside Aramaic formulae like imported machinery: the Tetragrammaton and its permutations, the self-naming of Exodus 3:14, Sabaoth, the explicit Name. Aramaic is the working prose and Hebrew supplies the levers. Much the same relation holds outside Judaism, which is what makes the comparison instructive. The Greek magical papyri and the Coptic texts of ritual power use Iaō, Sabaōth and Adōnai in the same way, as foreign names of power embedded in a vernacular frame, and they share with the bowls both the charaktēres, those ring-lettered signs with small circles at their terminals, and formations in which a word is written out and then shortened letter by letter down the page. An Aramaic handbook from the same world survives as the Sword of Moses, Ḥarba de-Moshe, published by Moses Gaster in 1896 and re-edited with a full study by Yuval Harari in 1997. What is set out here is the structure of these texts as the scholarship describes it; the operative sequences are deliberately not reproduced."],terms:[{native:"אשבעית עליכון",translit:"ashbaʿit ʿalekhon",gloss:"I adjure you (plural); from the root that also gives oath and seven"},{native:"מומתא",translit:"momata",gloss:"an oath; the thing by which the demon is bound rather than the demon itself"},{native:"עזקתא",translit:"ʿizqta",gloss:"signet-ring; the word used in Daniel 6:18 of the seal on the lions' den"},{native:"חתים",translit:"ḥatim",gloss:"sealed; the participle that closes a text, often doubled for emphasis"},{native:"אמן אמן סלה",translit:"amen amen selah",gloss:"the closing tag; Hebrew, and liturgical rather than magical in origin"},{native:"שם המפורש",translit:"shem ha-meforash",gloss:"the explicit Name. Hebrew, not Aramaic, and given here in its Hebrew form; the ultimate authority named after the word by"},{native:"רזא",translit:"raza",gloss:"mystery, secret; the word of Daniel 2, and the technical term for hidden knowledge"},{native:"עובדין בישין",translit:"ʿovadin bishin",gloss:"evil works; harm believed to come from a human enemy, listed beside demonic harm"}],texts:[{title:"Karl Preisendanz, Papyri Graecae Magicae (1928–31)",note:"The Greek magical papyri from Egypt; the comparative corpus without which the bowls' formulaic anatomy cannot be seen clearly."},{title:"Marvin Meyer and Richard Smith (eds), Ancient Christian Magic: Coptic Texts of Ritual Power (1994)",note:"The Coptic material in translation, sharing historiolae, charaktēres and names of power with the Aramaic texts."},{title:"Moses Gaster, The Sword of Moses (1896)",note:"Ḥarba de-Moshe, a magical handbook with substantial Aramaic; re-edited with a full study by Yuval Harari in 1997."},{title:"Joseph Naveh and Shaul Shaked, Magic Spells and Formulae (1993)",note:"Includes Geniza formularies, which show the model-book stage of composition that the bowls only imply."},{title:"Daniel 6:18",note:"The king seals the stone with his signet so that nothing be changed; the biblical Aramaic source of the sealing vocabulary."},{title:"Testament of Solomon",note:"Greek pseudepigraphon on Solomon's ring and his mastery of the demons; its date is disputed, but it is the literary background to the seal invoked on the bowls."}]},{slug:"early-jewish-mysticism",title:"Early Jewish Mysticism",native:"יורדי מרכבה",translit:"yordei merkavah",summary:"The Hekhalot literature's Aramaic strata, the adjuration of the Prince of the Torah, Metatron, and the argument that the ascent texts and the bowls share a technique. The terms here are Hebrew, being the corpus's own language.",paragraphs:["The Hekhalot corpus is mainly Hebrew, but Aramaic runs through it in strata, and where it appears it tends to appear in the adjurations. Passages of Hekhalot Zutarti are Aramaic; so is much of the material in the Geniza fragments that Peter Schäfer published in 1984; so are adjacent texts such as the Havdala de-Rabbi Aqiva. These works do not have authors or editions in the ordinary sense. Schäfer's Synopse of 1981 prints the manuscripts in parallel columns with numbered paragraphs precisely because there is no single work to edit: the tradition circulated, in his terms, as macroforms and microforms, blocks recombined by copyists who felt no obligation to a fixed text. Anyone approaching this literature expecting books will be disappointed, and the disappointment is itself the first datum.","What the practitioner took himself to be doing is described in the texts with unusual concreteness. The yored merkavah, the descender to the chariot, passes seven palaces; the idiom's inversion, descending in order to rise, has been much discussed and never settled. At each gate stand fierce guardians, and at each gate the traveller shows seals, which is to say names presented like passports. The danger is stressed constantly: the wrong seal at the sixth palace, the pure marble that looks like water — a motif that echoes the warning at Ḥagigah 14b — and the guardians who destroy those who cannot answer. Around this runs a mass of hymnic material addressed to the throne, of enormous repetitiveness, which some scholars read as descriptive of a state and others as the means of inducing it. Neither reading has carried the field.","One large body of the material has nothing to do with ascent. The Sar ha-Torah, the Prince of the Torah, is adjured so that learning will remain in the mind: the object is memory, not vision. Michael Swartz's Scholastic Magic reads this as the aspiration of a scribal class adjacent to the rabbinic academies rather than inside them, men who wanted what twenty years of study confers and did not have twenty years. The Sar ha-Panim, the Prince of the Countenance, is the inverse operation: the angel is brought down to the adept and compelled by the Name, and the texts themselves describe this as perilous, which is a fair indication of how their authors understood the relation between a name and the power it names.","Metatron stands at the centre of the corpus's most dangerous idea. He is the transformed Enoch of 3 Enoch, also called Sefer Hekhalot, a work whose date has been placed anywhere from the fifth to the ninth century; he is called the youth although he is the oldest of men, Prince of the Presence, bearer of seventy names, and in some texts the lesser bearer of the divine name. The scandal is registered by the tradition itself. At Ḥagigah 15a, Elisha ben Abuya sees him seated, since only God is seated in that court, and concludes that there are two powers in heaven; Metatron is given sixty lashes of fire to establish that he is a servant. The rabbis were not unaware of what their own angelology risked, and the story is their way of naming the risk and closing it.","The relation to the bowls is now widely accepted, though it was long resisted. Shaked argued in 1995, on the evidence of bowls that greet the angels and that quote liturgy close to Hekhalot hymns, that the two bodies of text share technique and very likely shared practitioners. Swartz found the same grammar of adjuration operating in both. Rebecca Lesses examined the ritual of adjuration as such, and Bohak's history placed Hekhalot adjuration inside the development of Jewish magic rather than in opposition to it. The old partition, in which mysticism is noble and magic vulgar, does not survive contact with the manuscripts, and least of all the Geniza manuscripts, in which ascent hymns and practical formulae for everyday needs stand side by side in the same hands."],terms:[{native:"היכלות",translit:"Hekhalot",gloss:"palaces (Hebrew); the seven halls of heaven, and the name of the literature describing them"},{native:"מרכבה",translit:"Merkavah",gloss:"chariot (Hebrew); the throne-vision of Ezekiel 1, and the object of the ascent"},{native:"יורדי מרכבה",translit:"yordei merkavah",gloss:"descenders to the chariot (Hebrew); the corpus's own name for its practitioners, with its unexplained inversion"},{native:"חותמות",translit:"ḥotamot",gloss:"seals (Hebrew); names shown to the gatekeepers of each palace, functioning as credentials"},{native:"שר התורה",translit:"Sar ha-Torah",gloss:"Prince of the Torah (Hebrew); the angel adjured for retention of learning rather than for vision"},{native:"שר הפנים",translit:"Sar ha-Panim",gloss:"Prince of the Countenance (Hebrew); the angel brought down to the adept, in a rite the texts themselves call dangerous"},{native:"מטטרון",translit:"Metatron",gloss:"the transformed Enoch, Prince of the Presence; the etymology of the name remains unresolved"},{native:"נער",translit:"naʿar",gloss:"youth (Hebrew); Metatron's title in 3 Enoch, given to the oldest of men"}],texts:[{title:"Peter Schäfer, Synopse zur Hekhalot-Literatur (1981)",note:"The manuscripts printed in parallel with numbered paragraphs; the edition that made the fluidity of the tradition visible rather than editing it away."},{title:"Peter Schäfer, Geniza-Fragmente zur Hekhalot-Literatur (1984)",note:"The Cairo Geniza fragments, which carry some of the corpus's oldest witnesses and much of its Aramaic."},{title:"Michael D. Swartz, Scholastic Magic: Ritual and Revelation in Early Jewish Mysticism (1996)",note:"The Sar ha-Torah material read as the ritual of a scribal class; the strongest account of why anyone adjured an angel for memory."},{title:`Shaul Shaked, "'Peace be Upon You, Exalted Angels': on Hekhalot, Liturgy and Incantation Bowls" (Jewish Studies Quarterly 2, 1995)`,note:"The article that set bowl texts beside Hekhalot hymns and argued for shared technique and shared practitioners."},{title:"Rebecca Lesses, Ritual Practices to Gain Power: Angels, Incantations, and Revelation in Early Jewish Mysticism (1998)",note:"Adjuration studied as a ritual form in its own right, across the ascent texts and the practical material alike."},{title:"Babylonian Talmud, Ḥagigah 14b–15a",note:"The four who entered the orchard, the warning about the water that is not water, and Aḥer's conclusion from seeing Metatron seated."}]},{slug:"aramaic-across-religions",title:"Aramaic Across the Religions",native:"ארמית",translit:"Aramit",summary:"One language serving Jews, Christians, Mandaeans, Manichaeans and pagans; three sibling dialects in one region; and a shared craft of protective writing that crossed lines theology had drawn hard.",paragraphs:["Aramaic had an imperial career before it had a religious one. As the chancery language of the Achaemenids it ran from the Indus to the Nile, and when the empire went the language stayed, fragmenting along lines of settlement and confession. The dialects belong to different periods and should not be flattened into one list: Nabataean and Palmyrene are earlier, epigraphic and largely pre-Christian, while the late western group comprises Jewish Palestinian, Christian Palestinian and Samaritan, and the late eastern group Syriac, Jewish Babylonian and Mandaic. Scripture itself records the transition. Ezra pauses to note that a letter was written in Aramaic, and Daniel switches into Aramaic in the middle of 2:4 and stays there to 7:28 before returning to Hebrew; a single Aramaic verse sits in Jeremiah 10:11, and two Aramaic words name a heap of stones at Genesis 31:47. No community ever owned this language, and the fact conditions everything that follows.","In late antiquity three siblings shared a region. Syriac, centred on Edessa, Christian, written in Estrangela and later in Serto and East Syriac hands. Jewish Babylonian Aramaic, in the square Hebrew script, the language of the Talmud. Mandaic, in an alphabet of its own that writes vowels with full letters, which is unusual among Semitic scripts and gives Mandaic manuscripts a degree of vocalisation that Syriac and Hebrew achieve only with added points. Grammatically these are close neighbours; Nöldeke, who wrote authoritative grammars of the Mandaic in 1875 and of Syriac in 1880, saw as much plainly, and Macuch later covered both classical and modern Mandaic. The deepest differences lie in script, in orthographic convention and in religious vocabulary rather than in the structure of the language. How far speakers could actually follow one another is not something the sources let us test, but the linguistic distance was small — which is one reason the confessional boundaries had to be maintained by other means.","Manichaeism belongs in this picture and is usually left out of it. Mani, born in Babylonia around 216 and raised in a baptising sect of the southern marshes described in the Cologne Mani Codex, wrote his scriptures in an Aramaic close to Syriac, and a distinctive script was used for them, related to the Palmyrene and Estrangela family; tradition credits Mani himself with devising it so that his books should not be corrupted in transmission as he believed his predecessors' had been, though the invention is a claim of the tradition rather than a demonstrated fact. A founder associated with an alphabet made to protect a canon is a striking figure in the history of religions all the same. A small number of incantation bowls are written in that script, which at the least places users of Manichaean writing in the same market for protective texts as everybody else.","And there was a shared technical culture. The ban attributed to Joshua bar Peraḥia turns up in Syriac as well as Jewish Aramaic. A Jewish bowl invokes Jesus; a Syriac Christian bowl invokes the seal of Solomon and Jewish angel names. In the Mandaean texts the old Mesopotamian gods survive as the seven planets and the children of Ruha — Šamiš and Sin, Nbu, Libat, Nirig, Bil and Kiwan, in the Drower and Macuch transliteration — their names worn down from the Akkadian Šamaš, Sîn, Nabû, Dilbat, Nergal, Bēl and Kayyamānu across two thousand years. Clients evidently commissioned from whoever was reputed effective. This is not evidence that the communities were indifferent to doctrine, for their polemics were precise and often savage. It suggests rather that beneath doctrine ran a conservative craft of names and writing which the polemics did not much touch, and which its practitioners may not have thought of as belonging to religion at all.","The language did not stop. Aramaic words are fossilised in Greek scripture, where the evangelist transliterates rather than translates at moments of intensity: the words spoken over Jairus's daughter at Mark 5:41, the cry from the cross, the word abba, and the invocation at the end of First Corinthians whose division into words is still argued. These survive only in Greek letters, and any Aramaic spelling of them is a modern retroversion. And Aramaic is spoken now. Ṭuroyo in Ṭur ʿAbdin and its diaspora; Assyrian and Chaldean Neo-Aramaic; the Jewish Neo-Aramaic dialects, now almost entirely in Israel; Western Neo-Aramaic at Maʿlula, Baxʿa and Jubbʿadin, whose speakers were scattered by the Syrian war; and Neo-Mandaic, spoken by a few hundred people chiefly of Khuzestani origin and documented by Häberl among others. Every one of these is endangered, several critically. This hall's texts are not a closed archive. They are the older layer of something still spoken, and in more than one community still prayed."],terms:[{native:"ארמית",translit:"Aramit",gloss:"in Aramaic; the word with which Ezra 4:7 marks a document as written in the imperial tongue"},{native:"לישנא",translit:"lishana",gloss:"tongue, language; the thing held in common while the scripts diverged"},{native:"כתבא",translit:"ktava",gloss:"writing, script, document; what actually distinguished the communities on the page"},{native:"אלהא",translit:"Elaha",gloss:"God; the same word in Jewish, Christian and Mandaean Aramaic alike, though written in three alphabets"},{native:"טליתא קומי",translit:"ṭlitha qumi",gloss:"little girl, arise; Mark 5:41 preserves this in Greek letters, and the Hebrew-script form given here is a scholarly retroversion"},{native:"מרנא תא",translit:"marana tha",gloss:"our Lord, come; the invocation of 1 Corinthians 16:22, transmitted in Greek letters as one word, its division still disputed. The form given here is a retroversion"},{native:"אבא",translit:"abba",gloss:"father; kept untranslated in the Greek New Testament at Mark 14:36 and in Paul, and given here in retroversion"}],texts:[{title:"Theodor Nöldeke, Mandäische Grammatik (1875)",note:"Written by a scholar who also produced the standard Syriac grammar and could see the two dialects as siblings; still the point of departure for Mandaic."},{title:"Michael Sokoloff, A Dictionary of Jewish Babylonian Aramaic of the Talmudic and Geonic Periods (2002)",note:"The standard lexicon for the dialect of the Talmud and of most of the bowls, drawing extensively on the magical corpus."},{title:"E. S. Drower and Rudolf Macuch, A Mandaic Dictionary (1963)",note:"The Mandaic lexicon, compiled with the community's priests, and the source of the transliteration used for Mandaic here and in the scholarly literature generally."},{title:"Ezra 4:8–6:18 and 7:12–26; Daniel 2:4b–7:28",note:"The Aramaic portions of the Hebrew Bible, with single verses at Jeremiah 10:11 and Genesis 31:47; the oldest continuous Aramaic most readers will have encountered."},{title:"The Cologne Mani Codex",note:"A Greek miniature codex on Mani's upbringing in a Mesopotamian baptising sect; the link between Manichaean origins and the baptist milieu of the south."},{title:"Marco Moriggi, A Corpus of Syriac Incantation Bowls (2014)",note:"The Christian bowls, in which Solomon's seal and Jewish angel names appear; the clearest documentation of the shared repertoire."}]}]};export{e as default};
